# Next.js 16 Standards

- Use Next.js 16.3 App Router APIs and conventions. Do not copy Next.js 14 patterns without checking the installed documentation in `node_modules/next/dist/docs/`.
- Explain each newly introduced Next.js 16 concept and compare it with the Next.js 14.1 approach in the implementation summary.
- Add concise learning comments only at the top or bottom of a file when a new Next.js 16 concept needs source-level context.

## Components and Rendering

- Choose Server and Client Components using current Next.js guidance and measured architectural trade-offs.
- Use Server Components for server-side data access, secrets, reduced client JavaScript, or streaming when they are the stronger choice.
- Use Client Components for state, events, effects, custom hooks, or browser APIs, and keep each `"use client"` module graph intentional.
- Choose static rendering, request-time rendering, streaming, or client rendering per route based on freshness, personalization, latency, and cacheability.
- Place runtime or uncached work behind meaningful Suspense boundaries when that preserves a useful static shell.
- Keep values passed to Client Components serializable and minimize the React Server Component payload.

## Async Request APIs and Routing

- Treat `params` and `searchParams` as promises and use generated `PageProps` or `LayoutProps` types.
- Always await `cookies()`, `headers()`, and other asynchronous request APIs.
- Read request-time values at the narrowest possible boundary so they do not make an entire route dynamic unnecessarily.
- Use App Router metadata APIs, route conventions, typed routes, and `next/image` or `next/font` when they are technically strongest.

## Cache Components

- Use Cache Components and `"use cache"` when they provide the best measured caching and rendering design.
- Give every cached scope intentional `cacheLife` and `cacheTag` semantics instead of relying on accidental defaults.
- Never access `cookies()`, `headers()`, or `searchParams` inside a cached scope; read them outside and pass serializable values in.
- Choose `updateTag`, `revalidateTag`, or `revalidatePath` according to read-your-own-writes versus stale-while-revalidate behavior.
- Account for deployment topology: default in-memory cache entries are not a globally durable cache across serverless instances or deployments.

## BFF and Request Handling

- Use Route Handlers as a thin BFF for secure aggregation, payload shaping, cookie handling, cache policy, and backend isolation.
- Keep business rules in the Express backend; do not duplicate domain logic in Next.js.
- Design BFF calls with explicit timeouts, cancellation, error mapping, observability, and minimal payloads.
- Use `proxy.ts`, not `middleware.ts`, for Next.js 16 request interception.
- Keep Proxy lightweight: redirects, rewrites, headers, experiments, and optimistic checks only. Do not perform slow fetching or full authorization there.

## Tooling

- Use Turbopack for development and production unless measured incompatibility requires Webpack.
- Run ESLint directly because `next lint` is removed.
- The React Compiler is disabled; do not enable it without an explicit measured decision.
